{"reason": "The initial plan already covers model architecture and training strategy changes. Historical experiments show that the current model suffers from overfitting (validation loss rises after epoch 3-4) due to random embedding initialization and simple pooling. The most impactful changes are: (1) replacing random word embeddings with pre-trained GloVe vectors to inject lexical-semantic priors, (2) introducing multi-head attention pooling to improve temporal aggregation. These are model design changes. Additionally, the learning rate schedule needs adjustment to prevent overfitting (OneCycleLR is known to stabilize training and improve convergence in NLP tasks). Therefore, I focus on 'model_design' and 'training_evaluation' modules.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Replace random word embedding initialization with GloVe 300d pre-trained vectors: load glove.6B.300d.txt from the input directory, build a word-to-vector map from training corpus, initialize nn.Embedding.weight using these vectors (keep unknown words as small random init, e.g., uniform -0.05 to 0.05), and add a 'freeze_epochs' attribute to freeze the embedding layer for the first 3 epochs. Also, replace the current mean+max pooling of BiLSTM outputs with a multi-head attention pooling layer: create an nn.Module with 4 attention heads, each computing a weighted sum of hidden states; concatenate head outputs and project down to 2*hidden_dim via a linear layer; integrate this into forward() to produce the 'bilstm_out'. This enhances the model's ability to focus on discriminative stylistic features, informed by successful use of attention in similar author attribution tasks.", "training_evaluation": "Replace the existing CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts scheduler (which shows validation loss stagnation/rise after epoch 3-4) with OneCycleLR scheduler. Set max_lr=1e-3, pct_start=0.3, div_factor=25, final_div_factor=1e4. Apply differential learning rates using parameter groups: embedding layers at 0.33x max_lr (to prevent catastrophic forgetting during early fine-tuning), backbone (BiLSTM+char convs) at 1x, feature projection + classifier at 3x. Additionally, to prevent overfitting observed in historical runs, increase label_smoothing from 0.1 to 0.2 and apply a higher dropout (0.4) in the classifier head. This schedule has been shown in similar experiments to stabilize convergence and avoid early overfitting (peak validation loss after epoch 4)."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The initial plan already covers model architecture and training strategy changes. Historical experiments show that the current model suffers from overfitting (validation loss rises after epoch 3-4) due to random embedding initialization and simple pooling. The most impactful changes are: (1) replacing random word embeddings with pre-trained GloVe vectors to inject lexical-semantic priors, (2) introducing multi-head attention pooling to improve temporal aggregation. These are model design changes. Additionally, the learning rate schedule needs adjustment to prevent overfitting (OneCycleLR is known to stabilize training and improve convergence in NLP tasks). Therefore, I focus on 'model_design' and 'training_evaluation' modules.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Replace random word embedding initialization with GloVe 300d pre-trained vectors: load glove.6B.300d.txt from the input directory, build a word-to-vector map from training corpus, initialize nn.Embedding.weight using these vectors (keep unknown words as small random init, e.g., uniform -0.05 to 0.05), and add a 'freeze_epochs' attribute to freeze the embedding layer for the first 3 epochs. Also, replace the current mean+max pooling of BiLSTM outputs with a multi-head attention pooling layer: create an nn.Module with 4 attention heads, each computing a weighted sum of hidden states; concatenate head outputs and project down to 2*hidden_dim via a linear layer; integrate this into forward() to produce the 'bilstm_out'. This enhances the model's ability to focus on discriminative stylistic features, informed by successful use of attention in similar author attribution tasks.",
    "training_evaluation": "Replace the existing CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts scheduler (which shows validation loss stagnation/rise after epoch 3-4) with OneCycleLR scheduler. Set max_lr=1e-3, pct_start=0.3, div_factor=25, final_div_factor=1e4. Apply differential learning rates using parameter groups: embedding layers at 0.33x max_lr (to prevent catastrophic forgetting during early fine-tuning), backbone (BiLSTM+char convs) at 1x, feature projection + classifier at 3x. Additionally, to prevent overfitting observed in historical runs, increase label_smoothing from 0.1 to 0.2 and apply a higher dropout (0.4) in the classifier head. This schedule has been shown in similar experiments to stabilize convergence and avoid early overfitting (peak validation loss after epoch 4)."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The initial plan's modifications (multi-sample dropout, SWA, higher label smoothing, linear warmup with shorter cosine cycle, CLS embedding dropout, feature noise injection) are well-founded based on the observed overfitting pattern. Historical experiments show that multi-sample dropout and SWA effectively reduce variance and flatten minima. The current code already has most of the training loop structure, so modifications should focus on implementing these changes precisely. The model_design module needs changes to add multi-sample dropout in forward pass and CLS dropout. The training_evaluation module needs changes to implement SWA, warmup + shorter cosine schedule, higher label smoothing, and feature noise injection. Data processing is not modified as no changes are needed there.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Modify forward method to implement multi-sample dropout: during training, run MC dropout with 5 forward passes and average logits (or use a dropout layer with training=True for inference averaging). Add a dropout layer (p=0.2) on the [CLS] token embedding before concatenation with features. Replace GELU with ReLU + 0.1 dropout in feature_proj. Ensure during inference, the model is set to train mode to enable dropout for Monte Carlo averaging.", "training_evaluation": "Implement Stochastic Weight Averaging (SWA) by saving model snapshots from the last 5 epochs and averaging them. Change label_smoothing from 0.1 to 0.2. Replace scheduler with linear warmup (first 3 epochs) followed by CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts(T_0=4). Add Gaussian noise (std=0.05) to feature embeddings during training only (apply noise to features tensor before forward pass). Use 5 forward passes at validation and test time with dropout enabled (set model.train() during inference) and average probabilities."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The initial plan's modifications (multi-sample dropout, SWA, higher label smoothing, linear warmup with shorter cosine cycle, CLS embedding dropout, feature noise injection) are well-founded based on the observed overfitting pattern. Historical experiments show that multi-sample dropout and SWA effectively reduce variance and flatten minima. The current code already has most of the training loop structure, so modifications should focus on implementing these changes precisely. The model_design module needs changes to add multi-sample dropout in forward pass and CLS dropout. The training_evaluation module needs changes to implement SWA, warmup + shorter cosine schedule, higher label smoothing, and feature noise injection. Data processing is not modified as no changes are needed there.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Modify forward method to implement multi-sample dropout: during training, run MC dropout with 5 forward passes and average logits (or use a dropout layer with training=True for inference averaging). Add a dropout layer (p=0.2) on the [CLS] token embedding before concatenation with features. Replace GELU with ReLU + 0.1 dropout in feature_proj. Ensure during inference, the model is set to train mode to enable dropout for Monte Carlo averaging.",
    "training_evaluation": "Implement Stochastic Weight Averaging (SWA) by saving model snapshots from the last 5 epochs and averaging them. Change label_smoothing from 0.1 to 0.2. Replace scheduler with linear warmup (first 3 epochs) followed by CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts(T_0=4). Add Gaussian noise (std=0.05) to feature embeddings during training only (apply noise to features tensor before forward pass). Use 5 forward passes at validation and test time with dropout enabled (set model.train() during inference) and average probabilities."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The execution shows clear overfitting (train loss 0.4050 vs val loss 0.6279) and a best log-loss of 0.3618, which matches the initial plan's diagnosis. Historical experiments show that combining extensive feature engineering with transformer fine-tuning can achieve better validation scores (e.g., 0.25-0.30 range) when proper regularization and data augmentation are applied. The three proposed changes are the most promising: data augmentation to increase effective training size, training regularization via self-distillation to reduce overfitting, and model architecture improvement to dynamically fuse features as informed by successful hybrid approaches in historical experiments. These modifications target the root causes without altering the successful backbone or feature engineering pipeline.", "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Add a back-translation augmentation step: for 50% of training samples per batch, generate a paraphrased version using a lightweight T5 model (t5-small). Integrate this into the SpookyDataset class to return both original and augmented texts, with a boolean flag indicating augmentation. Preserve all existing feature engineering, vectorizer, scaler, and data split logic unchanged. Use the same train/val stratified split indices and do not augment validation or test data.", "model_design": "Replace the simple concatenation + linear head with a gated cross-attention fusion: (1) a multi-head cross-attention layer where DeBERTa [CLS] is query and projected stylometric features are key/values, (2) a learnable gating vector to weight textual vs. stylometric contributions per sample, (3) a two-layer classification head with intermediate LayerNorm and stochastic depth (drop path probability 0.1). Keep the backbone (DeBERTa-v3-large, last 8 layers trainable), feature projection (64-dim), and dropout rate (0.3) unchanged. Ensure interface compatibility with existing forward signature.", "training_evaluation": "Replace standard CrossEntropyLoss with a self-distillation loss combining temporal ensembling and label smoothing: maintain a per-sample exponential moving average of predictions (momentum 0.99) as soft targets, use KL divergence between current predictions and ensemble for consistency, and add a small cross-entropy with smoothed labels (smoothing 0.1). Use a consistency weight of 0.5. Keep optimizer (AdamW), scheduler (CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts with warmup), gradient clipping, mixed precision, early stopping (patience 5), and batch sizes unchanged. Adjust warmup ratio to 0.15 to account for new loss terms."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The execution shows clear overfitting (train loss 0.4050 vs val loss 0.6279) and a best log-loss of 0.3618, which matches the initial plan's diagnosis. Historical experiments show that combining extensive feature engineering with transformer fine-tuning can achieve better validation scores (e.g., 0.25-0.30 range) when proper regularization and data augmentation are applied. The three proposed changes are the most promising: data augmentation to increase effective training size, training regularization via self-distillation to reduce overfitting, and model architecture improvement to dynamically fuse features as informed by successful hybrid approaches in historical experiments. These modifications target the root causes without altering the successful backbone or feature engineering pipeline.",
  "module": [
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering",
    "model_design",
    "training_evaluation"
  ],
  "plan": {
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Add a back-translation augmentation step: for 50% of training samples per batch, generate a paraphrased version using a lightweight T5 model (t5-small). Integrate this into the SpookyDataset class to return both original and augmented texts, with a boolean flag indicating augmentation. Preserve all existing feature engineering, vectorizer, scaler, and data split logic unchanged. Use the same train/val stratified split indices and do not augment validation or test data.",
    "model_design": "Replace the simple concatenation + linear head with a gated cross-attention fusion: (1) a multi-head cross-attention layer where DeBERTa [CLS] is query and projected stylometric features are key/values, (2) a learnable gating vector to weight textual vs. stylometric contributions per sample, (3) a two-layer classification head with intermediate LayerNorm and stochastic depth (drop path probability 0.1). Keep the backbone (DeBERTa-v3-large, last 8 layers trainable), feature projection (64-dim), and dropout rate (0.3) unchanged. Ensure interface compatibility with existing forward signature.",
    "training_evaluation": "Replace standard CrossEntropyLoss with a self-distillation loss combining temporal ensembling and label smoothing: maintain a per-sample exponential moving average of predictions (momentum 0.99) as soft targets, use KL divergence between current predictions and ensemble for consistency, and add a small cross-entropy with smoothed labels (smoothing 0.1). Use a consistency weight of 0.5. Keep optimizer (AdamW), scheduler (CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts with warmup), gradient clipping, mixed precision, early stopping (patience 5), and batch sizes unchanged. Adjust warmup ratio to 0.15 to account for new loss terms."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The current model overfits (train loss ~0.33, val loss rises after epoch 3-4, best val log loss ~0.674). The initial plan's core improvements (multi-head attention pooling, OneCycleLR scheduler, differential learning rates, increased label smoothing) address the root cause: mean+max pooling dilutes stylistic cues. Historical experiments confirm that attention mechanisms improve generalization on author attribution, and OneCycleLR with differential LR prevents overfitting. Model architecture and training strategy changes are most promising and directly implementable without altering the feature extraction pipeline or data split.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Replace the current mean+max pooling of BiLSTM outputs with a multi-head self-attention pooling mechanism. Add a MultiheadAttention layer (4 heads, embed_dim=hidden_dim*2) after the BiLSTM; use the learned attention to compute a weighted sum of hidden states across time steps. Concatenate the weighted sum with an additional max-pooled vector for final LSTM output. Keep the BiLSTM output dimension (hidden_dim*2) and char CNN pipeline unchanged. This allows the model to focus on position-dependent stylistic markers (e.g., rare word usage, sentence-initial conjunctions) as evidenced by successful similar experiments on author attribution.", "training_evaluation": "Replace CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts with OneCycleLR scheduler (max_lr=1e-3, pct_start=0.3, div_factor=25, final_div_factor=1e4) and apply differential learning rates: embedding layers at 0.33x (≈3.3e-4), BiLSTM+char convs at 1x (1e-3), feature projection+classifier at 3x (3e-3). Increase label_smoothing from 0.1 to 0.2. These changes are directly inspired by historical successful experiments where OneCycleLR with aggressive annealing and higher label smoothing prevented overfitting and improved generalization by converging to wider minima."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The current model overfits (train loss ~0.33, val loss rises after epoch 3-4, best val log loss ~0.674). The initial plan's core improvements (multi-head attention pooling, OneCycleLR scheduler, differential learning rates, increased label smoothing) address the root cause: mean+max pooling dilutes stylistic cues. Historical experiments confirm that attention mechanisms improve generalization on author attribution, and OneCycleLR with differential LR prevents overfitting. Model architecture and training strategy changes are most promising and directly implementable without altering the feature extraction pipeline or data split.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Replace the current mean+max pooling of BiLSTM outputs with a multi-head self-attention pooling mechanism. Add a MultiheadAttention layer (4 heads, embed_dim=hidden_dim*2) after the BiLSTM; use the learned attention to compute a weighted sum of hidden states across time steps. Concatenate the weighted sum with an additional max-pooled vector for final LSTM output. Keep the BiLSTM output dimension (hidden_dim*2) and char CNN pipeline unchanged. This allows the model to focus on position-dependent stylistic markers (e.g., rare word usage, sentence-initial conjunctions) as evidenced by successful similar experiments on author attribution.",
    "training_evaluation": "Replace CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts with OneCycleLR scheduler (max_lr=1e-3, pct_start=0.3, div_factor=25, final_div_factor=1e4) and apply differential learning rates: embedding layers at 0.33x (≈3.3e-4), BiLSTM+char convs at 1x (1e-3), feature projection+classifier at 3x (3e-3). Increase label_smoothing from 0.1 to 0.2. These changes are directly inspired by historical successful experiments where OneCycleLR with aggressive annealing and higher label smoothing prevented overfitting and improved generalization by converging to wider minima."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The current model shows overfitting (train loss ~0.53 vs val loss ~0.80 plateau) similar to the failed historical experiment. The initial plan's key direction is to replace shallow embeddings with a pretrained transformer (DistilBERT). However, historical experiments show that (1) a successful hybrid approach using DeBERTa-v3-large with extensive features achieved good results, while (2) a failed attempt with attention pooling and OneCycleLR still overfitted (val loss 0.67). To address the root cause of weak representations while leveraging pretrained models effectively, I will implement the transformer replacement (model_design) and adjust training (training_evaluation) to use conservative fine-tuning (differential LR) and avoid aggressive scheduling that caused early overfitting. Data processing remains unchanged as features are already engineered and the intent is to simplify them.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Replace the current word embedding, char embedding, BiLSTM, char CNN, and multihead attention modules with a single pretrained DistilBERT transformer encoder (distilbert-base-uncased). Use the [CLS] token output as the sentence representation. Optionally concatenate only the most discriminative 15-20 engineered features (e.g., author-specific word ratios, punctuation stats) via a learned linear projection (64-dim) before the classifier head. This eliminates the complex hybrid architecture and directly addresses the root cause of weak representations, as historical successful experiments with DeBERTa-v3-large showed that a transformer can capture nuanced stylistic differences. The model will have a forward method accepting 'input_ids', 'attention_mask', and optional 'features' (tensor of shape [batch, num_selected_features]), returning logits. The classifier head will be a two-layer MLP (hidden_dim=256, dropout=0.2) with LayerNorm and GELU.", "training_evaluation": "Switch to a conservative fine-tuning strategy: use a low learning rate (2e-5) for the DistilBERT backbone and a higher rate (2e-4) for the classifier/feature projection head. Replace OneCycleLR with a linear warmup (10% steps) followed by cosine decay to avoid aggressive annealing that historically caused overfitting. Increase label smoothing from 0.2 to 0.3 to further regularize. Use AdamW with weight decay (0.01) and gradient clipping (max_norm=1.0). Keep the 5-fold CV and early stopping (patient 5) but train for up to 10 epochs as transformers converge faster. This balances fine-tuning with regularization to prevent the validation loss plateau observed in both the current and failed historical experiments."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The current model shows overfitting (train loss ~0.53 vs val loss ~0.80 plateau) similar to the failed historical experiment. The initial plan's key direction is to replace shallow embeddings with a pretrained transformer (DistilBERT). However, historical experiments show that (1) a successful hybrid approach using DeBERTa-v3-large with extensive features achieved good results, while (2) a failed attempt with attention pooling and OneCycleLR still overfitted (val loss 0.67). To address the root cause of weak representations while leveraging pretrained models effectively, I will implement the transformer replacement (model_design) and adjust training (training_evaluation) to use conservative fine-tuning (differential LR) and avoid aggressive scheduling that caused early overfitting. Data processing remains unchanged as features are already engineered and the intent is to simplify them.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Replace the current word embedding, char embedding, BiLSTM, char CNN, and multihead attention modules with a single pretrained DistilBERT transformer encoder (distilbert-base-uncased). Use the [CLS] token output as the sentence representation. Optionally concatenate only the most discriminative 15-20 engineered features (e.g., author-specific word ratios, punctuation stats) via a learned linear projection (64-dim) before the classifier head. This eliminates the complex hybrid architecture and directly addresses the root cause of weak representations, as historical successful experiments with DeBERTa-v3-large showed that a transformer can capture nuanced stylistic differences. The model will have a forward method accepting 'input_ids', 'attention_mask', and optional 'features' (tensor of shape [batch, num_selected_features]), returning logits. The classifier head will be a two-layer MLP (hidden_dim=256, dropout=0.2) with LayerNorm and GELU.",
    "training_evaluation": "Switch to a conservative fine-tuning strategy: use a low learning rate (2e-5) for the DistilBERT backbone and a higher rate (2e-4) for the classifier/feature projection head. Replace OneCycleLR with a linear warmup (10% steps) followed by cosine decay to avoid aggressive annealing that historically caused overfitting. Increase label smoothing from 0.2 to 0.3 to further regularize. Use AdamW with weight decay (0.01) and gradient clipping (max_norm=1.0). Keep the 5-fold CV and early stopping (patient 5) but train for up to 10 epochs as transformers converge faster. This balances fine-tuning with regularization to prevent the validation loss plateau observed in both the current and failed historical experiments."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The initial plan proposes a switch to a Transformer-based architecture (DistilBERT) to address the plateau in validation loss (~0.674) and overfitting. The current BiLSTM+handcrafted features approach shows consistent overfitting (train loss ~0.33 vs val loss >0.65). Similar historical experiments confirm that transformer-based models significantly outperform BiLSTM-based methods on author attribution, with successful implementations using DeBERTa-v3-large with feature engineering. However, one experiment that used explicit author keyword features (poe_words, lovecraft_words, shelley_words) failed due to label leakage. Therefore, I focus on data_processing_and_feature_engineering to remove leaking features and control vocabulary size, and model_design to implement the DistilBERT-based architecture with proper feature integration.", "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design"], "plan": {"data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "WHAT: Remove the hand-crafted author-specific keyword features (poe_word_ratio, lovecraft_word_ratio, shelley_word_ratio, lovecraft_long_desc, poe_gothic) as they constitute explicit label leakage and cause overfitting to surface patterns. Additionally, reduce the number of engineered features to only the most predictive ones to avoid noise; keep basic statistics, vocabulary richness, POS ratios, readability scores, sentiment features, n-gram diversity, and structural features but drop redundant ones. WHY: Historical failed experiments show that using author keyword ratios leads to overfitting and poor generalization; the initial plan explicitly advises removing these features. Successful experiments used a broad set of stylometric features but not explicit author keywords. This change forces the model to learn genuine stylistic differences. HOW: Modify the feature extraction functions to exclude the `extract_author_vocab_features` function entirely or comment it out. Also, apply feature selection by removing low-variance features or using mutual information to select top 50 features from the remaining pool. Preserve the existing variable names and scaling pipeline. Ensure that training-set statistics (median for imputation, scaler) are fitted only on training data to prevent leakage.", "model_design": "WHAT: Replace the current hybrid BiLSTM+CharCNN+feature model with a DistilBERT-based architecture. Load `distilbert-base-uncased` from HuggingFace Transformers, freeze the first 4 layers, fine-tune layers 5-6 plus classification head. Keep all engineered features from the cleaned set but concatenate them via a learned projection (64-dim) with the [CLS] token embedding (768-dim). Use a 2-layer MLP classifier (832->256->128->3) with LayerNorm, GELU, dropout 0.3, and label smoothing 0.15. WHY: The initial plan identifies that the current architecture's bottleneck is the limited capacity of BiLSTM and manual features. Transformers automatically capture contextual and stylistic patterns, and the feature projection provides complementary signals. Successful experiments used similar hybrid approaches with DeBERTa and achieved significant improvements. HOW: In the `model_design` module, define a new class `SpookyTransformerClassifier` that uses `AutoModel.from_pretrained('distilbert-base-uncased')`. Implement a forward method that processes text through DistilBERT, obtains the pooled [CLS] output, projects engineered features to 64 dimensions, concatenates them, and passes through the MLP classifier. Set up differential learning rates: 1x for unfrozen transformer layers (2e-5), 0.1x for frozen layers (though not updated), 10x for feature projection and classifier heads (2e-4, 2e-4). Preserve the existing training loop interface but update dataset classes to handle the new tokenizer. Use the existing `SpookyDataset` but replace tokenization with DistilBERT tokenizer (max_length=256, truncation=True, padding='max_length')."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The initial plan proposes a switch to a Transformer-based architecture (DistilBERT) to address the plateau in validation loss (~0.674) and overfitting. The current BiLSTM+handcrafted features approach shows consistent overfitting (train loss ~0.33 vs val loss >0.65). Similar historical experiments confirm that transformer-based models significantly outperform BiLSTM-based methods on author attribution, with successful implementations using DeBERTa-v3-large with feature engineering. However, one experiment that used explicit author keyword features (poe_words, lovecraft_words, shelley_words) failed due to label leakage. Therefore, I focus on data_processing_and_feature_engineering to remove leaking features and control vocabulary size, and model_design to implement the DistilBERT-based architecture with proper feature integration.",
  "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design"],
  "plan": {
