    "model_design": "WHAT: Replace the current hybrid BiLSTM+CharCNN+feature model with a DistilBERT-based architecture. Load `distilbert-base-uncased` from HuggingFace Transformers, freeze the first 4 layers, fine-tune layers 5-6 plus classification head. Keep all engineered features from the cleaned set but concatenate them via a learned projection (64-dim) with the [CLS] token embedding (768-dim). Use a 2-layer MLP classifier (832->256->128->3) with LayerNorm, GELU, dropout 0.3, and label smoothing 0.15. WHY: The initial plan identifies that the current architecture's bottleneck is the limited capacity of BiLSTM and manual features. Transformers automatically capture contextual and stylistic patterns, and the feature projection provides complementary signals. Successful experiments used similar hybrid approaches with DeBERTa and achieved significant improvements. HOW: In the `model_design` module, define a new class `SpookyTransformerClassifier` that uses `AutoModel.from_pretrained('distilbert-base-uncased')`. Implement a forward method that processes text through DistilBERT, obtains the pooled [CLS] output, projects engineered features to 64 dimensions, concatenates them, and passes through the MLP classifier. Set up differential learning rates: 1x for unfrozen transformer layers (2e-5), 0.1x for frozen layers (though not updated), 10x for feature projection and classifier heads (2e-4, 2e-4). Preserve the existing training loop interface but update dataset classes to handle the new tokenizer. Use the existing `SpookyDataset` but replace tokenization with DistilBERT tokenizer (max_length=256, truncation=True, padding='max_length')."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The current validation score (0.4598 with MC dropout) indicates overfitting: training loss steadily decreased while validation loss plateaued after epoch 4. Successful historical experiments with LSTMs plus attention pooling and differential LRs improved generalization. Failed experiments with aggressive OneCycleLR led to sharp increases in val loss. The initial plan's core direction (unfreeze embeddings, linear warmup+decay, LLRD, higher dropout) aligns with historical best practices. The most impactful changes are in model architecture (embedding unfreezing, LLRD, dropout) and training strategy (scheduler refinement, reduced warmup). Data processing is already extensive and unchanged in the plan. So I focus on model_design and training_evaluation modules.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Add full unfreezing of embedding layer (word_embeddings and LayerNorm) by setting model.backbone.deberta.embeddings.requires_grad_(True). Embedding layer parameters captured in a new param group with lowest LR (1e-5) via LLRD: embeddings=1e-5, bottom 8 encoder layers=2e-5, top 8 encoder layers=5e-5, head=8e-5. Keep backbone encoder frozen for layers beyond last 8. Increase dropout in classification head from 0.1 to 0.2 and add dropout_p=0.1 after feature projection ReLU. This enriches low-level token representations while preventing overfitting through differential LRs.", "training_evaluation": "Replace the manual linear warmup (3 epochs) + CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts with a sequential scheduler: use LinearLR (start_factor=0.1, total_iters=3 epochs) for warmup, then CosineAnnealingLR (T_max=8 epochs, eta_min=1e-6) for decay, then restart with CycleLR (T_0=3) to allow 3-epoch cycles. Add ReduceLROnPlateau (factor=0.5, patience=2) monitoring val loss to handle plateaus. Also, adjust the number of SWA snapshots to last 3 epochs (instead of 5) since the validation plateau suggests unstable last epochs. This balances exploration and exploitation, preventing abrupt LR jumps that hurt validation in failed experiments."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The current validation score (0.4598 with MC dropout) indicates overfitting: training loss steadily decreased while validation loss plateaued after epoch 4. Successful historical experiments with LSTMs plus attention pooling and differential LRs improved generalization. Failed experiments with aggressive OneCycleLR led to sharp increases in val loss. The initial plan's core direction (unfreeze embeddings, linear warmup+decay, LLRD, higher dropout) aligns with historical best practices. The most impactful changes are in model architecture (embedding unfreezing, LLRD, dropout) and training strategy (scheduler refinement, reduced warmup). Data processing is already extensive and unchanged in the plan. So I focus on model_design and training_evaluation modules.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
